I see you're running into a couple of issues. Let's fix them:
1. Fix the Cargo.toml Error
You need to add the crate-type configuration to your Cargo.toml file. Open your Cargo.toml file and add this section:
toml
[lib]
crate-type = ["cdylib", "rlib"]
This tells the Rust compiler to generate both a C-compatible dynamic library (needed for WebAssembly) and a Rust library format.
2. Install a Basic HTTP Server
Instead of basic-http-server, you can use a simpler alternative:
bash
# Option 1: If you have Python installed
python -m http.server --directory static

# Option 2: Using <REDACTED> install -g http-server
http-server static

# Option 3: Using cargo install
cargo install simple-http-server
simple-http-server static
3. Your Complete Setup Flow
Here's the full set of commands you should run:
bash
# 1. Make sure your Cargo.toml has the crate-type configuration
echo '[lib]
crate-type = ["cdylib", "rlib"]' >> Cargo.toml

# 2. Build the WASM module
wasm-pack build --target web --out-dir static/pkg

# 3. Start a web server (using Python as an example)
python -m http.server --directory static
4. Add Event Listeners for Value Display
I notice the HTML has <span> elements for displaying the slider values, but your JS doesn't update them. Add this to your static/main.js file right before the window.onload line:
javascript
// Update value displays when sliders change
function setupValueDisplays() {
    document.getElementById('complexity').addEventListener('input', function() {
        document.getElementById('complexityValue').textContent = this.value;
    });

    document.getElementById('speed').addEventListener('input', function() {
        document.getElementById('speedValue').textContent = parseFloat(this.value).toFixed(1);
    });

    document.getElementById('scale').addEventListener('input', function() {
        document.getElementById('scaleValue').textContent = parseFloat(this.value).toFixed(1);
    });

    document.getElementById('colorMode').addEventListener('input', function() {
        document.getElementById('colorModeValue').textContent = this.value;
    });
}

// Add this to your initialize function
function initialize() {
    // ... existing code ...

    // Set up UI controls
    document.getElementById('regenerateBtn').addEventListener('click', regenerateShader);
    document.getElementById('shaderType').addEventListener('change', regenerateShader);
    setupValueDisplays();
}
Everything else in your code looks good! Your HTML, Rust, and JavaScript implementations match the expected structure. Once you make these changes, your WebAssembly shader generator should work properly.

